# Caddyfile for the local production box (10.100.100.86).
#
# APPLY BY: copying to /opt/gcgc-tms/Caddyfile, then
#   sudo podman restart gcgc-tms_caddy_1
# Not applied yet — it needs someone on the GCGC network.
#
# ── The problem this fixes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The current Caddyfile serves ONLY the hostname `tms.local`:
#
#   { auto_https disable_redirects }
#   tms.local { tls internal; reverse_proxy app:3000 }
#
# Caddy therefore listens on 443 only, and answers only to that SNI/Host. So:
#   http://10.100.100.86/       -> nothing listening on :80        (connection fails)
#   https://10.100.100.86/      -> no matching site for the IP     (connection fails)
#   https://tms.local/          -> WORKS, but `tms.local` is NXDOMAIN on the
#                                  network, so nobody can actually use it
#
# The only usable address is http://10.100.100.86:3000, published directly by
# the app container. That is why users must remember a port number, and it is
# the reachability half of the "TMS can only be used on the GCGC network"
# report — the other half is the LAN itself, which is not a Caddy problem.
#
# ── What this changes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Adds a catch-all HTTP site so the bare IP works on port 80. `http://` on a
# site address tells Caddy to serve plain HTTP and not attempt a certificate —
# essential here, because a publicly-trusted cert cannot be issued for a private
# IP, and that is exactly why bare-IP HTTPS fails today.
#
# `tms.local` is kept unchanged, so the moment an internal DNS record (or the
# tms.hotelsogo.com decision) exists, HTTPS works with no further edits.
#
# Port 3000 stays published by the app container regardless, so the current
# address keeps working and this change cannot cut anyone off.

{
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# Named host — already works, waiting on DNS.
tms.local {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy app:3000
}

# Catch-all over plain HTTP: makes http://10.100.100.86/ work without DNS and
# without a port. `http://` prevents Caddy trying to get a certificate for it.
http:// {
	reverse_proxy app:3000
}
